  "notes": "The most imitated Memphis vocal template, and the parameters that carry it are the kick skeleton and a bright motif over a dark bed. The research gives the kick note by note — 808 on 1 and the \"&\" of 2 and 3 — so `anchors` takes beat 1, `secondaryAnchor` the \"&\" of 2 and `offbeat8thShare` carries the rest; that is a denser floor than the parent's two-a-bar. Harmonic movement is described as effectively zero (i, or i–VI) with the hook melody carrying all the interest, so `impliedByRiff` is set and the chord part's variety comes from `chordDurationBeats` and a 32-semitone voicing register rather than from progressions he does not play. The melodic layer is authored as the research describes it — a bright marimba/bell ostinato against minor pads, deliberately narrow: `motifPitchCount` 3–5 mirrors the 4–5 semitone band the chanted hooks sit in. ⚠ `medium` rather than high: the research rates it high-medium and marks the tempo band **[LOW-CONF]** — `session.bpm` is lane-anchored to the 78 BPM crunk figure (70–82 half-time = 140–164 straight), not measured from his records. Every other parameter here traces to a described element. ⚠ The novelty/vocal percussion the research names (the clucking figures on \"Chickenhead\") is modelled as an extra `perc` lane rather than a sound, because the engine writes notes and not samples. ⚠ The bass ceiling came down from 40 to 39 so it stays under a chord part that starts at 40 — `coherence.rs` requires the highest bass note to sit at or below the lowest chord note, and this model overlapped by 1. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
